Falsifies `system_tests::user::c_seam_tests::a_c_out_of_bounds_write_faults_and_changes_nothing_outside_its_grant`.

This is the worked example milestone 202's block names, and it is here rather than
under a crate's `falsifications/` because the test is a `#[test_case]` that boots
under QEMU and not a Kani harness. **The path is provisional and nothing sweeps
it**: `script/falsifications` walks `crates/` and keys on `#[kani::proof]`, and
DECISIONS §134's convention does not reach a kernel test. The proposal that would
is in design/roadmap/0202-confinement-claims-falsified.md, and it needs a way to
run one kernel test by name, which today does not exist.

The defect: `c_confiner` maps `WITNESS_RO` into the C component's address space
read/**write** instead of read-only. Nothing else changes: the same C runs, the
same deliberate off-by-one store executes, the same checker reads the same bytes
through the confiner's own mappings afterwards. What changes is whether the MMU was
ever asked the question.

**Why this falsification is the one worth having.** DECISIONS §31 asserts that
after the C component's out-of-bounds write, two witness pages are unchanged. That
passes in two very different worlds: the component reached the page and the
hardware stopped it, or the component never got near the address and the assertion
is decorative. `WITNESS_RO` is deliberately the *same physical frame* the confiner
holds read/write, so an unchanged page is supposed to mean "reachable, and the
store did not happen". This patch is how that sentence gets checked.

Expected to fail: `assert!(wait_for_report(report), "report {i} of
{EXPECTED_REPORTS} never arrived...")` in `run_seam`. Confirmed: it fires at
report 4 of 12, which is attempt 1's first report, and the suite stops there.

**And that is not the assertion anyone would have guessed, which is this record's
real content.** The obvious answer is the verdict equality, `assert_eq!(v[2],
CONFINED, "attempt {attempt}: the C component was not confined...")`, the
assertion that prints all four bits including `read-only witness intact`. It never
runs, and the measured shape is worth stating exactly. Attempt 0 still produces
all four of its reports, so its verdict *is* computed, and by construction it
cannot be `CONFINED`: the store now lands in `WITNESS_RO` before running off the
end of that page into the address the confiner deliberately leaves unmapped, so
both `WITNESS_RO_INTACT` and `FAULT_ADDR_AS_EXPECTED` are false. Attempt 1 then
never reports at all, and `run_seam` collects every report before the test looks
at any of them, so the run dies at the collection and the verdict assertion is
never evaluated.

The general fact underneath that: a component which is not confined does not
fault, a component that does not fault produces no death report, and a run
missing a death report stalls. So **the witness check, which is the sentence §31
leads with, is not what catches a broken confinement in practice; the absence of
a fault is.** The witness bits are reached only by an escape that faults anyway,
and this design makes that hard to construct.

**Measured twice, 2026-08-31, `script/test --arch aarch64`.** The first run was
against `run_seam`'s original blocking receive, and it went red as a **watchdog
timeout at 234 seconds** reading `a livelock, not a lost wakeup`: right answer,
useless diagnostic, and exactly the wrong-reason red milestone 202's block warns
about. `wait_for_report` was added in the same lane so the stall is reported where
it means something, and the second run is the one this record names.

